-- title:
--   Wiebe's lemma on colon ideals of (f)=g· x
-- statement:
--   Let $P$ be a commutative ring, let $n$ be a natural number, let $x, f : \mathrm{Fin}\ n \to P$ be two families of $n$ elements of $P$, and let $g$ be an $n \times n$ matrix over $P$ indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\ n$. Assume that $f = g\,x$ entrywise, i.e. $f_i = \sum_j g_{ij} x_j$ for every $i$, and assume that there exists a family $y : \mathrm{Fin}\ n \to P$ such that the list $y_0,\dots,y_{n-1}$ is a weakly regular sequence on $P$ in the sense of `RingTheory.Sequence.IsWeaklyRegular` (each $y_i$ acts as a non-zero-divisor on $P$ modulo the ideal generated by the earlier terms) and such that $y_i$ lies in the ideal generated by the range of $f$ for every $i$. Writing $(f)$ and $(x)$ for the ideals spanned by the ranges of $f$ and of $x$, the conclusion is the conjunction of two equalities of ideals of $P$: the colon ideal $(f) : (x)$ equals $(f) + (\det g)$, the supremum of $(f)$ and the principal ideal generated by $\det g$; and the colon ideal $(f) : (\det g)$ equals $(x)$. No regularity hypothesis is placed on $x$ or on $f$ themselves, only the existence of some weakly regular sequence of length $n$ inside $(f)$.
--
--   This is Wiebe's transition lemma, which computes colon ideals (and hence annihilators and socles) of a complete intersection presented by a matrix factorisation $f = g\,x$; it belongs to the commutative-algebra input to the Wiles–Lenstra numerical criterion for complete intersections. In this development it is used to identify the annihilator of the kernel of a map from a power-series ring with the ideal generated by $\det g$, in [`MvPowerSeries.annihilator_ker_eq_span_det`](thm.html#MvPowerSeries.annihilator_ker_eq_span_det) and [`MvPowerSeries.quotient_mk_det_mem_of_ne_bot`](thm.html#MvPowerSeries.quotient_mk_det_mem_of_ne_bot).
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Ideal_colon_span_eq_sup_span_det_of_isWeaklyRegular.lean

import Mathlib.RingTheory.Regular.RegularSequence
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Colon
import Mathlib.RingTheory.MvPowerSeries.Basic
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Determinant.Basic

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false

universe u

theorem Ideal.colon_span_eq_sup_span_det_of_isWeaklyRegular
    {P : Type u} [CommRing P] {n : ℕ} (x f : Fin n → P) (g : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) P)
    (hf : ∀ i, f i = ∑ j, g i j * x j)
    (hy : ∃ y : Fin n → P, RingTheory.Sequence.IsWeaklyRegular P (List.ofFn y) ∧
      ∀ i, y i ∈ Ideal.span (Set.range f)) :
    (Ideal.span (Set.range f)).colon (Ideal.span (Set.range x)) =
        Ideal.span (Set.range f) ⊔ Ideal.span {g.det} ∧
      (Ideal.span (Set.range f)).colon (Ideal.span {g.det}) = Ideal.span (Set.range x) := by sorry
